{
 "id": "2026-10-25-half-life-halvings",
 "topic": "half-life: why 10 half-lives leave about 0.1% of a sample",
 "subject": "chemistry",
 "hook_type": "exam pain",
 "caption": "First-order decay loses the same fraction in every equal stretch of time, so the half-life never depends on how much you start with. Ten halvings leave 1/1024 of the sample: about 0.1%.\n\nSave this for your next kinetics exam.\n\n#chemistry #kinetics #halflife #radioactivity #physics #calculus #stem #science #exams",
 "cover_at": 1.0,
 "hook": "How long until 99.9% of a radioactive sample is gone?",
 "sub": "Count halvings, not seconds.",
 "beats": [
  {
   "text": "Every nucleus has the same small chance of decaying each second. So the decay rate is proportional to what's left.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "e1",
     "tex": [
      "\\frac{dN}{dt}",
      "=",
      "-k",
      "N"
     ],
     "size": 110,
     "at": [
      0,
      3.4
     ],
     "colors": {
      "0": "yellow",
      "3": "blue"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Solve that, and the amount left shrinks exponentially.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "e2",
     "tex": [
      "N(t)",
      "=",
      "N_0",
      "e^{-kt}"
     ],
     "size": 100,
     "next_to": "e1",
     "buff": 0.8,
     "colors": {
      "0": "blue",
      "2": "teal"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Now ask: when has it halved? Set $N = N_0/2$.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "e3",
     "tex": [
      "\\tfrac{1}{2}",
      "N_0",
      "=",
      "N_0",
      "e^{-k t_{1/2}}"
     ],
     "size": 92,
     "next_to": "e2",
     "buff": 0.8,
     "colors": {
      "1": "teal",
      "3": "teal"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Notice that $N_0$ cancels. The half-life doesn't care how much you start with.",
   "do": [
    {
     "indicate": "e3",
     "color": "teal"
    },
    {
     "hide": "e3",
     "time": 0.4
    },
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "tex": [
      "t_{1/2}",
      "=",
      "\\frac{\\ln 2}{k}"
     ],
     "size": 100,
     "colors": {
      "0": "yellow"
     },
     "next_to": "e2",
     "buff": 0.8,
     "name": "e4"
    },
    {
     "circle": "e4"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "do": [
    {
     "clear": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "So every half-life does the same thing: it cuts whatever remains in half.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "text",
     "name": "ttl",
     "text": "% of the sample left",
     "size": 52,
     "color": "dim",
     "at": [
      0,
      5.6
     ]
    },
    {
     "make": "bars",
     "name": "br",
     "values": [
      100,
      50,
      25,
      12.5,
      6.25,
      3.125
     ],
     "labels": [
      "0",
      "1",
      "2",
      "3",
      "4",
      "5"
     ],
     "colors": [
      "blue"
     ],
     "max": 100,
     "step": 25,
     "size": [
      7.2,
      4.0
     ],
     "at": [
      0,
      3.1
     ]
    },
    {
     "make": "text",
     "name": "xl",
     "text": "half-lives",
     "size": 48,
     "color": "dim",
     "next_to": "br",
     "buff": 0.25
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Fifty, twenty-five, twelve and a half... After ten halvings, you're left with one part in 1024.",
   "do": [
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "f1",
     "tex": [
      "\\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{10}",
      "=",
      "\\frac{1}{1024}"
     ],
     "size": 88,
     "at": [
      0,
      -0.9
     ],
     "colors": {
      "0": "yellow"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "That's about 0.1% left. So 99.9% is gone after roughly ten half-lives.",
   "do": [
    {
     "sfx": "riser"
    },
    {
     "make": "tex",
     "name": "f2",
     "tex": [
      "10",
      "\\text{ half-lives}",
      "\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad",
      "99.9\\%",
      "\\text{ gone}"
     ],
     "size": 54,
     "colors": {
      "0": "yellow",
      "3": "green"
     },
     "next_to": "f1",
     "buff": 0.45
    },
    {
     "sfx": "ding"
    },
    {
     "make": "box",
     "name": "bx",
     "around": "f2"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Same trick for any first-order process: seven halvings is about 1%, twenty is about one in a million.",
   "do": [
    {
     "hide": "bx",
     "time": 0.3
    },
    {
     "indicate": "f1",
     "color": "yellow"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
